// The section "make" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Make() {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>make(T)</code> is the value of a type, which the compiler computes, so no type is
            left in the JS: <code>make(Song)</code> is <code>{'{ title: "", seconds: 0 }'}</code>,
            {" "}<code>make(Song[])</code> is <code>[]</code>, <code>make(Seconds)</code> of
            {" "}<code>type Seconds number</code> is <code>0</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            An interface gives an object of its fields, each with its own value; a field that may
            be missing, <code>?T</code> or <code>any</code>, is left out. A literal type gives its
            literal, <code>Map</code> and <code>Set</code> are empty, and a class whose constructor
            takes nothing is <code>new Cart()</code>. Each <code>make</code> is a new value. A
            union, a function, an interface with methods and a type that is its own field have no
            value of their own: the compiler says why.
        </p>
        <p>
            In a generic function <code>make(T)</code> is the value of the type of each call,
            {" "}<code>{"fill<Song>(2)"}</code> or one inferred from the arguments. A function called
            with one type gets the value in its body; one called with several, or exported, takes a
            function that makes it before its own parameters; one never called is left out of the
            JS with a warning. <code>{"decode<T>"}</code> takes its type the same way
            (<a href="../decode/">decode</a>).
        </p>
        <Example name="make" />
    </>
}
